\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 4: Generalized Method of Moment (GMM) Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
Violation of the Gauss-Markov Assumption - Non-Exogenous Regressors: 
\series default
Population (or theoretical) moment conditions and sample (empirical) moment
 conditions; Estimation method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the MME in the 
\emph on
exactly (just) 
\emph default
identified case, in which the number of unknown parameters is the same as
 the number of sample moment equations, there is a unique solution for the
 parameters.
 In some cases we may have more population moment conditions than the number
 of parameters, so that the system of sample moment conditions is 
\emph on
overidentified
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
overdetermined
\emph default
.
 For example, consider the example of the MME of the standard linear regression
 model.
 We used the weak exogeneity condition 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{t})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose we wish to use the assumption of strong exogeneity of regressors,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{s})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we can use the theoretical moments conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{t})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}|X_{t-1})=0$
\end_inset

, which imply 
\begin_inset Formula $E(X_{t}^{\prime}u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(X_{t-1}^{\prime}u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This gives a system of 
\begin_inset Formula $2K$
\end_inset

 equations for 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 unknown parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and there is no solution that satisfies all moment conditions.
 The generalized method of moment (GMM) is designed to estimate the parameters
 in such a case.
 Of course, the case of just-identifed moment conditions is a specail case
 of the GMM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To introduce the ideas of the GMM, we will discuss the GMM estimator of
 a linear regression model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=Z_{t}\theta_{0}+u_{t},\ t=1,\ldots T\implies y=Z\theta_{0}+u
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 parameter value, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times p$
\end_inset

 vector of regressors, some of which may be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The covariance matrix of the error vector is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(u|X)=(\omega_{ts})=\Omega$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 can be a scalar matrix (homoskedasticity and no serial correlations), a
 diagonal matrix (heteroskedasticity and no serial correlations), or a general
 symmetrix positive definite matrix (heteroskedasticity and serial correlations).
 We denote 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 the set of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 (weakly) exogenous variables, which inlude all exogenous variable in 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Some notations are listed below for an easy reference.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
m_{t}=X_{t}^{\prime}u_{t}=X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta)\\
\mu_{t}(\theta_{0})\equiv E(m_{t})=E(X_{t}^{\prime}u_{t})=E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0})\right]=0\\
\bar{m}(\theta)=\frac{1}{T}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}m_{t}=\frac{1}{T}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}X_{t}^{\prime}u_{t}=\frac{1}{T}X^{\prime}u=\frac{1}{T}X^{\prime}(y-Z\theta)\\
\bar{\mu}(\theta_{0})=\frac{1}{T}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\mu_{t}(\theta_{0})=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identifiability of Parameters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 gives the population moment condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}(\theta_{0})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This says that the moment condition is satisfied by the true value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 of the parameter.
 Is there another value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 that satisfies the moment condition? Parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
identifialbe by the population moment condition
\emph default
 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

 has a unique solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and the solution is equal to the true value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 If there is at least one other solution 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 that satisfies the population moment condition, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}(\theta_{1})=0$
\end_inset

, then parameter vectore 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
unidentifable by the population moment condition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is not identifiable and there are more than one 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 that satisfies the population moment condition besides the true parameter,
 we would not know which parameter we estimate by using the sample moment
 that matches the poulation moment condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What conditions are required to assue identifiability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 by the population moment condition? By adding ad substracting, we can rewrite
 the moment condition as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu_{t}(\theta) & = & E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}\left(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta\right)\right]\\
 & = & E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}\left(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0}\right)\right]+E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}\left(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta\right)\right]-E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}\left(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0}\right)\right]\\
 & = & 0+E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}y_{t}\right]-E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]\theta-E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}y_{t}\right]+E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]\theta_{0}\\
 & = & 0-E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]\left(\theta-\theta_{0}\right)=0\\
 & = & E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z\right]\left(\theta-\theta_{0}\right)=0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The last expression indicates that the solution for this moment condition
 occurs at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]$
\end_inset

 has full column rank, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]=p$
\end_inset

.
 If this rank condition is satisfied, the only solution to the equation
 above is 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta_{0}-\theta)=0$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]<p$
\end_inset

, there is a non-zero vector 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]c=0$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta_{0}-\theta)=c$
\end_inset

.
 This gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{0}-c\neq\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]$
\end_inset

 has a dimension of 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times p$
\end_inset

, the 
\emph on
necessary condition
\emph default
 for the full column rank is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\geq p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*
The necessary condition (order condition) for the identifiability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 by the population moment condition is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\geq p$
\end_inset

.
 The necessary and sufficient condition (rank condition) for the identifiability
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 by the population moment condition is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t}\right]=p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the rank condition is satisfied, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
just-identified
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $K=p$
\end_inset

, and 
\emph on
over-identified
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $K>p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
GMM Estimation Procedure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is identifiable, the GMM finds its estimate from the sample moment condition
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

.
 This is a system of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 equations in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 unknown parameters.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p=K$
\end_inset

, it is a just-identified system and 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

 is a nonsingular square matrix.
 Hence, we can compute 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{m}(\theta)=\frac{1}{T}X^{\prime}(y-Z\theta)=0\implies X^{\prime}y-X^{\prime}Z\theta=0\implies\hat{\theta}=(X^{\prime}Z)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

This estimator is called the instrumental variable (IV) estimator and 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is called the instrumental variables.
 This estimator gives a perfect fit of the sample moment condition and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is identically zero for all elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case of overidentifying moment conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $(K>p)$
\end_inset

 there is no solution that satisfies all moment conditions.
 That is, we cannot find an estimator such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}(\hat{\theta})=0$
\end_inset

 for all elements.
 All that we can do is one of the following two approaches:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
to reduce the number of moment conditions to the number of the just identified
 case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
to find an estimator that makes a certain measure of the distance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 from zero as small as possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
First Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the first approach, we reduce the overidentified system to the just-identifie
d system by selecting 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 number of sample moment conditions and then solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times p$
\end_inset

 selection matrix with full column rank.
 If we wish to pick just the first 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 conditions, we specify 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{\prime}=(I_{p},0)$
\end_inset

.
 This particular selection matrix is not a sensible choice as we discar
 some of the moment conditions and picking the first 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 conditions is arbitrary.
 A more sensible approach is to find the 
\series bold
best
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 linearly independent linear combinations of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 moment conditions, where the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 selection matrix is the selection matrix that leads to the best estimator
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Premultiplying 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 to the sample moment conditions, we have the just identified 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{m}(\theta)=F^{\prime}\bar{m}(\theta)=\frac{1}{T}(XF)^{\prime}(y-Z\theta)=\frac{1}{T}(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}y-F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\theta)=0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

 is a square matrix of full rank, the solution to this set of conditions
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\theta}=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}y=\theta+\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}u
\]

\end_inset

with an asymptotic covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta}|X)=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\left(Z^{\prime}XF\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, how do we find the best selection matrix? Note that the variance and
 covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 depends on the choice of weighting matrix.
 This suggests that we wish to choose 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 that yields the highest precision of the estimator, that is, the smallest
 covariance matrix.
 It is straightworward to prove that the optimal weighting matrix is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F=\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z
\]

\end_inset

This choice gives the estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}=\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

with the asymptotic covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta}|X)=\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The difference in the covariances, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is postive semidefinite for any choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Method 1
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1}=Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}\Omega X=D^{\prime}D$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1} & = & Z^{\prime}X\left(D^{\prime}D\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}\left[I_{K}-DF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}D^{\prime}F^{\prime}\right]D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}\left[I_{K}-DF\left(F^{\prime}D^{\prime}DF\right)^{-1}D^{\prime}F^{\prime}\right]D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A=DF$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{A}=I_{K}-A(A^{\prime}A)^{-1}A^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{A}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and idempotent.
 Thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1} & = & BM_{A}B^{\prime}\\
 & = & BM_{A}M_{A}^{\prime}B^{\prime}\\
 & = & CC^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C=BM_{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Method 2
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\left(Z^{\prime}XF\right)^{-1}-\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}\Omega X=DD^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}DD^{\prime}F\left(Z^{\prime}XF\right)^{-1}-\left[Z^{\prime}XD^{\prime-1}D^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $A=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}D$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{\prime}=D^{\prime}F\left(Z^{\prime}XF\right)^{-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=Z^{\prime}XD^{\prime-1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{\prime}=D^{-1}X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})=AA^{\prime}-\left[BB^{\prime}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

From the BLUE proof, if 
\begin_inset Formula $AB=I$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $AA^{\prime}-\left[BB^{\prime}\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
2SLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Premultiply 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}$
\end_inset

to the regression model under study to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X^{\prime}y=X^{\prime}Z\theta+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=X^{\prime}u$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\epsilon)=X^{\prime}\Omega X$
\end_inset

.
 The GMM estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the GLS estimator of this transformed regression model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If the error term as a scalar covariance matrix, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(u|X)=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

, the the GMM estimator becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}_{2SLS}=\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

This estimator is called the 
\emph on
two-stage least squares (2SLS) estimator
\emph default
 because it can be obtained in two stages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Stage 1: Regress 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, and obtain the predicted values of 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Z}=X\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

; Stage 2: Regress 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Z}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}_{2SLS}=\left(\hat{Z}^{\prime}\hat{Z}\right)^{-1}\hat{Z}^{\prime}y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

